@node Overview
@section Emacsvox Overview

Emacsvox provides a small number of core services around which the
remainder of the audio interface is constructed.  These essential
features of the software are briefly outlined in the following
paragraphs; the commands by which they can be controlled will be
described later in the manual.

Apart from providing a fluent spoken interface to all of Emacs' basic
editing functions, Emacsvox also includes software modules which add
speech feedback to a range of applications that can be run from within
Emacs. In this sense, Emacsvox amounts to much more than a talking
text editor; indeed, it can more aptly be characterized as a true
``audio desktop'', in which speech is treated as a first-class output modality.

Emacsvox implements a special minor mode, known as ``voice lock mode''
(@pxref{Voice-lock}) which uses distinct speech characteristics to
provide aural highlighting of specific textual constructs, such as
comments in program code, quoted strings and reserved words @xref{ Voice
Lock}. This facility is further extended when Emacsvox is used with the
@code{EWW} and @code{W3} Web browsers, to enable the semantic and
structural distinctions captured by the HTML markup to be communicated
efficiently @xref{Web Browsing}.

It is often desirable to exercise control over the pronunciation of a
word (E.G. a technical term or a reserved word in a programming
language) within specific contexts. Emacsvox maintains pronunciation
dictionaries for this purpose, which may be customized by the
user. Moreover, individual dictionaries can be activated selectively,
depending for example on the current major mode or the name of the
file which is being visited @xref{emacsvox-pronounce}.

In addition to spoken feedback, Emacsvox can generate ``auditory
icons'' @MDash{} short sound cues which alert the user to significant
events, for example the opening or deletion of a file, the completion
of an action, the arrival of an electronic mail message or the
creation of a completion buffer. Sound cues act as a supplement to the
spoken interface, and are especially valuable to the experienced user
in facilitating rapid interaction. Note that in order to support
auditory icons, the computer must be equipped with sound hardware for
which the operating system has been correctly configured
@xref{emacsvox-sounds}.

@node Working
@section  Using Emacs Buffers

While typing in an Emacs buffer, hitting space speaks the recently
typed word.  I use completion all the time; so Emacsvox will speak
the completion just inserted as well as the next possible completion.
In Emacs, use @kbd{M-x load-library @key{RETURN} completion
@key{RETURN}} for loading the completion package.

The standard Emacs prompting functions have also been speech-enabled.
Emacs prompts with available lists of completions in response to partial
input wherever appropriate  @MDash{} all forms of completion provide speech
feedback.

In addition, Emacsvox provides a number of commands for reading
portions of the current buffer, getting status information, and
modifying Emacsvox's state.

All of the commands are documented in the subsequent sections.
They can be classified into types:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Emacsvox commands for listening to chunks of information.  Most of
these command names start with @code{emacsvox-speak-}.  Their default
bindings are grouped under the main Emacsvox prefix @kbd{C-e}@footnote{The
prefix is inherited from Emacspeak, where C-e was mnemonic for the
project name.}.  The starter bindings are introduced in @ref{Reading};
the live Help system and generated key reference remain authoritative
when bindings are customized.

@item
The second category manipulates the speech engine.  These commands
normally start with @code{tts-} and are grouped under @kbd{C-e d}
@footnote{Historically, d was mnemonic for DECtalk; the prefix now
contains engine-independent speech controls.}.
@end itemize

Emacs and Emacsvox both expose their current commands through Emacs Help.
Use the small set below to get oriented, then use Help or the generated
reference instead of relying on a copied exhaustive list.

@node Reading
@section Reading and Navigating

Ordinary Emacs movement speaks the text crossed by point: moving by a
character, word, line, or paragraph presents the corresponding unit.
The commands below let you inspect useful units without first moving.
They use the default @kbd{C-e} prefix.

This is deliberately a starter set, not a second complete key reference.
If a binding below differs in your session, use @kbd{C-h k} on that key
and check your customizations.  The complete generated defaults are in
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps}.

@c basic-usage-live-keys-begin
@table @kbd

@findex emacsvox-speak-char
@kindex C-e c
@item C-e c
@code{emacsvox-speak-char}
speaks the character at point.

@findex emacsvox-speak-word
@kindex C-e w
@item C-e w
@code{emacsvox-speak-word}
speaks the current word.  Repeating it at the same position spells the
word.

@findex emacsvox-speak-line
@kindex C-e l
@item C-e l
@code{emacsvox-speak-line}
speaks the current line.

@findex emacsvox-speak-paragraph
@kindex C-e [
@item C-e [
@code{emacsvox-speak-paragraph}
speaks the current paragraph.

@findex emacsvox-speak-page
@kindex C-e ]
@item C-e ]
@code{emacsvox-speak-page}
speaks the current page.

@findex emacsvox-speak-region
@kindex C-e r
@item C-e r
@code{emacsvox-speak-region}
speaks the active region between point and mark.

@findex emacsvox-speak-rest-of-buffer
@kindex C-e i
@item C-e i
@code{emacsvox-speak-rest-of-buffer}
speaks from point to the end of the buffer.

@findex emacsvox-speak-buffer
@kindex C-e b
@item C-e b
@code{emacsvox-speak-buffer}
speaks the entire current buffer.

@findex tts-stop
@kindex C-e s
@item C-e s
@code{tts-stop}
stops current speech.

@findex emacsvox-toggle-show-point
@kindex C-e C-d
@item C-e C-d
@code{emacsvox-toggle-show-point}
toggles the aural indication of point within spoken text.

@end table
@c basic-usage-live-keys-end

Many speaking commands accept a prefix argument to limit speech to the
text before or after point.  Use @kbd{C-h k} on a command before relying
on those variants, because their exact argument behavior is documented
with the command.
@node Speech System


@section Speech System Commands
@include tts.texi

@node Voice-lock
@section Voice Lock Mode

Toggle voice lock globally with @kbd{M-x}
@code{global-voice-lock-mode}.  Its current default binding is in
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps}.

Voice-lock is turned on automatically in all buffers that support it
when Emacsvox is started.

Emacsvox can be set to disable voice lock in all of the major modes
that support it. To do so, insert the following statement into your
Emacs initialization file:

@lisp
(global-voice-lock-mode -1)
@end lisp


The characteristics of the different voice personalities used by voice
lock mode vary according to the capabilities of the speech
synthesizer. The definitions applicable to the Dectalk family of
synthesizers are contained in @file{dectalk-voices.el}, which is
supplied as part of the Emacsvox distribution. The Emacsvox
distribution also contains voice definitions for many other
synthesizers.

Using voice lock mode, Emacsvox also supports many of the aural style
properties defined in level 2 of the World Wide Web Consortium's
Cascading Style Sheet specification (see
@url{http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-CSS2/}. Thus, when Emacsvox is running
in conjunction with a cooperating user agent, such as William Perry's
Emacsvox/W3 web browser, the rendering of HTML documents can be
regulated by style sheets. Examples of style rules which employ the
CSS audio properties can be found in the default style sheet which is
supplied in the Emacs/W3 distribution.


@node Status Information
@section Status  Commands


These common commands answer frequent orientation questions.  Less common
status commands are listed in @ref{Emacsvox Keymaps}.

@c basic-usage-live-keys-begin
@table @kbd

@findex emacsvox-speak-message-again
@kindex C-e a
@item C-e a
@code{emacsvox-speak-message-again}

Speak the last message from Emacs once again.

@findex emacsvox-speak-mode-line
@kindex C-e m
@item C-e m
@code{emacsvox-speak-mode-line}

